+
+# Translator trust, distribution and interoperability evidence
+
+<Callout type="warning">
+**Draft companion proposal for Signet 2.** This document separates protocol conformance, package trust, integration authority and empirical game compatibility so they can be evaluated independently.
+</Callout>
+
+## Why this exists
+
+A public translator is executable integration software.
+
+Its trust story is broader than "the package is signed" and broader than "the translator passes the protocol tests".
+
+Several claims that sound similar require different evidence:
+
+~~~text
+ROLE_CONTRACT
+!= SANDBOX_POLICY
+
+DECLARED_PERMISSION
+!= ENFORCED_PERMISSION
+
+VALID_SIGNATURE
+!= CURRENT_AUTHORIZATION
+
+SAFE_ARTIFACT
+!= PROTOCOL_CONFORMANCE
+
+PROTOCOL_CONFORMANCE
+!= GAME_COMPATIBILITY
+
+SELF_CONFORMANCE
+!= PAIRWISE_INTEROPERABILITY
+!= INDEPENDENT_REPRODUCTION
+!= ECOSYSTEM_ADOPTION
+~~~
+
+This proposal gives each claim a separate place.
+
+## Integration mode is part of the trust subject
+
+A translator's authority depends on how it integrates with the target.
+
+Examples:
+
+~~~text
+HOST_PROCESS_EXTENSION
+SANDBOXED_WORKER
+CONTROLLED_SERVER_GATEWAY
+EXTERNAL_OFFICIAL_API
+REIMPLEMENTATION
+FULL_APPLICATION
+~~~
+
+These are not security-equivalent.
+
+A native engine extension can execute with the host process's authority.
+
+A controlled-server gateway may instead hold administrative network credentials.
+
+An external official API can have a narrower brokered surface.
+
+Package metadata should identify the integration mode being claimed.
+
+Compatibility evidence should be scoped to that mode.
+
+## Role contracts describe need, not enforcement
+
+An adapter contract can say that a translator needs operations such as:
+
+~~~text
+HOST
+IMPORTER
+WORLD
+INPUT
+PRESENTATION
+AUTHORITY
+~~~
+
+That is useful for review and code generation.
+
+It does not prove that the implementation is technically prevented from doing anything else.
+
+~~~text
+ROLE_CONTRACT != SANDBOX_POLICY
+~~~
+
+A complete deployment may use one or more enforcement layers:
+
+- a broker/API that exposes only approved operations;
+- a separate process;
+- an OS sandbox;
+- filesystem restrictions;
+- network restrictions;
+- scoped credentials;
+- platform-specific entitlements;
+- user-visible permission prompts.
+
+The protocol should report declared permissions and enforced confinement separately.
+
+## Package identity
+
+A published translator package should have an immutable artifact identity.
+
+At minimum:
+
+~~~text
+artifact_digest
+package_name
+package_version
+publisher_identity
+integration_mode
+target_game_identity
+target_integration_identity
+semantic_profile_compatibility
+~~~
+
+An artifact digest identifies bytes.
+
+It does not identify the full effective profile or prove current authorization.
+
+## Signature versus authorization
+
+A cryptographically valid signature answers:
+
+> which key signed these bytes?
+
+It does not always answer:
+
+> is that key still authorized to publish this package now?
+
+~~~text
+VALID_SIGNATURE != CURRENT_AUTHORIZATION
+~~~
+
+The distribution design should therefore support:
+
+- trust-root identity;
+- delegated publisher roles;
+- expiration;
+- revocation;
+- key rotation;
+- compromise recovery;
+- rollback protection where appropriate.
+
+A package can remain historically well-signed after the signing key loses current authorization.
+
+## Provenance and reproducibility
+
+Useful package claims include:
+
+- source revision;
+- build recipe;
+- builder identity;
+- dependency identities;
+- build provenance;
+- reproducibility status;
+- artifact digest.
+
+These claims should be displayed independently.
+
+A reproducible build can strengthen confidence that published bytes correspond to reviewed source.
+
+It does not replace runtime confinement or compatibility testing.
+
+## Effective profile identity
+
+Runtime behavior can change without package bytes changing.
+
+Examples:
+
+- participant overlay changes;
+- calibration changes;
+- semantic profile changes;
+- session policy changes;
+- a typed fallback becomes active.
+
+Therefore compatibility evidence should be able to reference:
+
+~~~text
+package artifact digest
++
+effective profile digest
++
+session/ruleset identity
++
+integration mode
+~~~
+
+## Compatibility lifecycle
+
+"Latest" is not a sufficient package lifecycle.
+
+Useful states include:
+
+~~~text
+ACTIVE
+SUPERSEDED
+DEPRECATED
+YANKED_COMPATIBILITY
+COMPATIBILITY_INVALIDATED
+SECURITY_REVOKED
+PUBLISHER_REVOKED
+LEGAL_OR_POLICY_BLOCKED
+~~~
+
+These states mean different things.
+
+A compatibility regression is not necessarily malware.
+
+A key compromise is not the same as a policy disagreement.
+
+A legal support boundary is not a cryptographic failure.
+
+## Evidence scopes
+
+A single PASS label should not cover every interoperability claim.
+
+A useful evidence ladder is:
+
+~~~text
+V0  STRUCTURAL_VALIDITY
+V1  BEHAVIORAL_CONFORMANCE
+V2  INDEPENDENT_IMPLEMENTATION
+V3  PAIRWISE_INTEROPERABILITY
+V4  INDEPENDENT_REPRODUCTION
+V5  OPERATIONAL_FRESHNESS
+~~~
+
+### V0: structural validity
+
+Examples:
+
+- manifest parses;
+- schema validates;
+- required fields exist;
+- canonical digest reproduces.
+
+### V1: behavioral conformance
+
+The implementation passes normative semantic/adapter test vectors.
+
+### V2: independent implementation
+
+A separately developed implementation reproduces the normative behavior.
+
+This reduces the chance that one reference implementation accidentally defines the protocol by itself.
+
+### V3: pairwise interoperability
+
+Two real target integrations successfully exercise the same session contract together.
+
+### V4: independent reproduction
+
+A third party reproduces the claimed experiment from the published artifacts and procedure.
+
+### V5: operational freshness
+
+The evidence is still current for the declared game version, integration surface, profile cut and dependencies.
+
+## Mapping topology versus validation demand
+
+A semantic hub is valuable because translator implementation topology approaches:
+
+~~~text
+O(n)
+~~~
+
+rather than pairwise bridge authoring.
+
+That does not imply that all empirical validation becomes linear.
+
+~~~text
+O(n) mapping topology
+!=
+O(n) empirical validation demand
+~~~
+
+Two translators can each pass local conformance and still interact badly because of:
+
+- timing;
+- ordering;
+- visibility;
+- fallback composition;
+- lifecycle semantics;
+- authority boundaries;
+- target-specific bugs.
+
+The protocol should therefore distinguish architectural scaling from empirical evidence scope.
+
+## Scoped compatibility records
+
+A compatibility result should identify exactly what was tested.
+
+Illustrative record:
+
+~~~json
+{
+  "translator_artifact": "sha256:...",
+  "effective_profile": "sha256:...",
+  "target": {
+    "game": "example-game",
+    "version": "1.2.3",
+    "integration_mode": "CONTROLLED_SERVER_GATEWAY"
+  },
+  "semantic_contract": "sha256:...",
+  "evidence_level": "V3_PAIRWISE_INTEROPERABILITY",
+  "peer_target": {
+    "game": "other-game",
+    "version": "4.5.6"
+  },
+  "procedure": "https://example.org/reproduction/...",
+  "observed_at": "2026-10-05T00:00:00Z"
+}
+~~~
+
+Do not upgrade a narrow harness result into a wider runtime claim.
+
+For example:
+
+~~~text
+ABI_HOST_HARNESS_PASS
+!=
+FULL_RUNTIME_INTEROPERABILITY_PASS
+~~~
+
+## Preserve negative evidence
+
+Failed experiments are protocol evidence.
+
+Do not erase a failed result when a later design succeeds.
+
+Useful negative records include:
+
+- unsupported operation;
+- incomplete adapter role coverage;
+- semantic mismatch;
+- ordering conflict;
+- stale profile evidence;
+- pairwise regression;
+- no-match resolver case.
+
+A failed generic adapter interface can be more useful than an optimistic pass because it identifies what the abstraction omitted.
+
+## Profile-only changes are supply-chain changes
+
+A profile pull request can change behavior while executable package bytes stay identical.
+
+Review tooling should surface:
+
+- semantic subject;
+- changed authority class;
+- old/new semantic relation;
+- shared-state impact;
+- invalidated compatibility evidence;
+- required replay/conformance reruns;
+- new immutable profile digest.
+
+~~~text
+PACKAGE_BYTES != EFFECTIVE_PROFILE
+~~~
+
+## Forge should be local-first
+
+Signet Forge does not need to be part of the real-time protocol path.
+
+A conservative deployment model is:
+
+~~~text
+runtime:
+  deterministic pinned profile
+
+Forge:
+  local development/calibration tool by default
+
+remote resolver:
+  optional and explicit
+
+per-tick remote model:
+  not required
+~~~
+
+If a hosted resolver is used, its export boundary should be visible before data leaves the machine.
+
+## Remote export contract
+
+A hosted Forge/resolver request should be describable by an explicit schema.
+
+The user should be able to know whether the request contains:
+
+- semantic identifiers;
+- candidate identifiers;
+- descriptions;
+- game/version identity;
+- profile identity;
+- calibration summaries;
+- any participant-specific metadata.
+
+The default should minimize exported data.
+
+## Local approval versus dataset contribution
+
+A person can approve a local mapping without agreeing to publish it as training data.
+
+~~~text
+LOCAL_APPROVAL != DATASET_CONTRIBUTION
+~~~
+
+Dataset contribution should be a separate explicit action.
+
+A contributed example should record:
+
+- immutable example ID;
+- contribution license;
+- provenance;
+- semantic/profile version;
+- candidate set;
+- chosen value;
+- creation time;
+- supersession or invalidation lineage.
+
+## Dataset invalidation
+
+Mappings can later be found incorrect or stale.
+
+A dataset should be able to answer:
+
+- which training example came from that mapping;
+- whether it has been superseded;
+- which model builds included it;
+- whether a corrected replacement exists.
+
+Deleting history is usually less useful than explicit invalidation and lineage.
+
+## Resolver and dataset authority
+
+A model suggestion is not protocol authority.
+
+~~~text
+MODEL_SUGGESTION != AUTHORITY
+~~~
+
+Dataset popularity is also not protocol authority.
+
+A frequently selected mapping can still be semantically wrong for a particular version, integration surface or ruleset.
+
+Human review should resolve against the normative semantic contract, not merely majority frequency.
+
+## Governance roles
+
+As the ecosystem grows, several governance powers should remain distinguishable.
+
+### Protocol governance
+
+Owns:
+
+- wire contract;
+- normative semantic identity rules;
+- core versioning;
+- compatibility rules.
+
+### Semantic-profile governance
+
+Owns:
+
+- profile definitions;
+- conformance assertions;
+- profile versioning;
+- deprecation/supersession.
+
+### Translator/profile publisher authority
+
+Owns:
+
+- translator implementation;
+- publisher baseline;
+- integration-specific mappings;
+- release process.
+
+### Participant authority
+
+Owns:
+
+- participant overlay;
+- local input binding;
+- local appearance choices;
+- local privacy/export choices.
+
+### Package-directory authority
+
+Owns:
+
+- listing;
+- namespace policy;
+- evidence display;
+- compatibility yanks;
+- directory moderation.
+
+### Update-root authority
+
+Owns:
+
+- trust roots;
+- publisher delegation;
+- key recovery;
+- revocation infrastructure.
+
+### Security incident authority
+
+Owns:
+
+- emergency quarantine;
+- advisory publication;
+- security restoration criteria.
+
+### Forge/dataset/model governance
+
+Owns:
+
+- dataset admission;
+- contribution policy;
+- example invalidation;
+- model release identity.
+
+### Conformance authority
+
+Owns:
+
+- test-suite revisions;
+- PASS semantics;
+- evidence-level definitions;
+- reproduction requirements.
+
+The same people can initially occupy several roles.
+
+The artifacts should still identify which authority made which decision.
+
+## Legal and project-policy scope
+
+An official integration support policy is a project governance choice.
+
+It should not be presented as a universal conclusion about every jurisdiction, game license or interoperability exception.
+
+A useful state is:
+
+~~~text
+LEGAL_OR_POLICY_BLOCKED
+~~~
+
+with the scope/reason recorded separately from technical or security state.
+
+## First trust/validation experiment
+
+A small joint experiment can exercise these boundaries.
+
+Use two materially different target integrations, for example:
+
+- one open-engine native integration;
+- one controlled-server gateway.
+
+For each, publish:
+
+- package digest;
+- integration mode;
+- declared permissions;
+- enforced confinement where present;
+- semantic contract digest;
+- effective profile digest;
+- V0/V1 evidence.
+
+Then run:
+
+1. independent semantic-contract reproduction;
+2. pairwise runtime interoperability;
+3. dependency drift;
+4. profile-only behavior change;
+5. publisher update with participant-overlay preservation;
+6. current-authorization revocation;
+7. compatibility invalidation;
+8. independent reproduction.
+
+The experiment can succeed by finding a missing boundary.
+
+A falsified abstraction is useful if the result is preserved and the specification becomes narrower and more precise.
+
+## Compact invariants
+
+~~~text
+ROLE_CONTRACT
+!= SANDBOX_POLICY
+
+DECLARED_PERMISSION
+!= ENFORCED_PERMISSION
+
+VALID_SIGNATURE
+!= CURRENT_AUTHORIZATION
+
+PACKAGE_BYTES
+!= EFFECTIVE_PROFILE
+
+MODEL_SUGGESTION
+!= AUTHORITY
+
+LOCAL_APPROVAL
+!= DATASET_CONTRIBUTION
+
+SELF_CONFORMANCE
+!= PAIRWISE_INTEROPERABILITY
+!= INDEPENDENT_REPRODUCTION
+!= ECOSYSTEM_ADOPTION
+~~~
+
+Each distinction exists because the two sides require different evidence or different authority.
+
